\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Brazil economy seen weathering Russian woes. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tracey Ober \par \uc2 743 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 1998\par 20:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  RIO DE JANEIRO, Aug 17 (Reuters) - Brazil's emerging economy is likely to keep steaming along and suffer no more than a ripple of spillover from Russia's effective \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation and partial debt moratorium, officials and economists said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  After Russia widened its rouble trading band and imposed a 90-day halt to some debt repayments early Monday, Brazil's equity and bond markets stumbled - though the setbacks were relatively mild - while its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, seemed to be holding its own. \par \par \uc2  To be sure, traders were a bit jumpy after the news from Moscow, but Brazilian officials rushed to reassure the local financial markets that Russia's crisis would have minimum impact on Latin America's largest economy. \par \par \uc2  Brazilian Finance Minister Pedro Malan said the moves that Russia took on Monday to strengthen its fragile financial system would not hurt his country. \par \par \uc2  "There is no reason for this to have any effect on Brazil," Malan said at a banking conference. \par \par \uc2  He went even further to suggest that Brazil could continue its policy of gradually reducing interest rates despite renewed concern about the health of emerging markets worldwide. \par \par \uc2  "I hope that this trajectory of declining (rates) continues," Malan said at the conference in Porto Alegre, capital of the southern Brazilian state of Rio Grande do Sul. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  nearly doubled interest rates late last year, jacking them up to 43 percent to protect the real from speculative attack after the financial crisis in Asia escalated in October. Since then, it has brought them back down to an annual 19.75 percent. \par \par \uc2  Malan said the economy was in a better position now than last October to withstand global market pressures, noting that foreign direct investment would climb this year to $21 billion from $17 billion in 1997. \par \par \uc2  Economists were generally in agreement with this official line, saying Brazil's external accounts were in very good shape compared with Russia's. \par \par \uc2  "Brazil is in a completely different situation than Russia," Marcelo Serfaty, economist and head of the equity group at Brazilian bank Pactual, said. \par \par \uc2  "There are no big sell-offs or any crisis. There is a very limited volume of orders with everybody waiting to see what happens. Of course the level of risk in emerging markets has increased, but that's a question of price not of viability." \par \par \uc2  He said there was an adjustment in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market after the Russian news but saw no major pressure and no reason to raise interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's Bovespa index of leading shares dropped more than 2 percent Monday to 8546 points at 1630 GMT, but traders said the index ought to stabilise. \par \par \uc2  At the same time, Brazil's "C" bonds were down 4 points at 65.50 percent, and the real was holding steady near 1.1730 to the dollar, after rumours of indirect \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped relieve some initial pressure. \par \par \uc2  Economists said \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  would stay in line with other emerging markets because of the high liquidity of its assets, rather than any underlying flaw in the economy. \par \par \uc2  Brazil was also helped by a stable political situation in which President Fernando Henrique Cardoso, the father of a inflation-slashing plan that turned the economy around in recent years, was expected to have no difficulty winning re-election on Oct. 4. \par \par \uc2  An massive privatisation programme was another factor fuelling the economy, economists said. \par \par \uc2  "The money flowing out is offset by privatisation money," Serfaty said. "There is no major pressure in the short term, but there needs to be a cut in taxes on fixed-income investments to reduce interest rates further." \par \par \uc2  Malan said sharp declines in the prices of dollar-denominated Brazilian Brady and Eurobonds over the past two weeks in New York and London had not reflected a negative outlook on Brazil. Rather investors had sold off Brazilian fixed-income assets because they were the most easily traded emerging market securities, he said. \par \par \uc2  "It's obvious that since we live in a global era, there will be some effects in the debt market. But those who need liquidity will sell off their Brazilian debt because ours are the most liquid securities," Malan said. \par \par \uc2  "There is nothing to sustain the idea that this is a reflection on Brazil's economy." ((-Rio de Janeiro newsroom +55 21 507 4151, fax ext 2120, tracey.ober@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8h00xk0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
