\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil Forex \highlight\uc2 : Real Steadies; Talk Of  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 174 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 1998\par 22:28\par \uc2  Emerging Markets Report \par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--The Brazilian real (BRL) opened weaker Thursday but then firmed slightly and remained steady during the remainder of the day, traders said. \par \par \uc2  One Rio de Janeiro trader said the federal bank Banco do Brasil was selling dollars on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the real from slipping further. \par \par \uc2  He said the Banco do Brasil spent some $200 million to bring the dollar to BRL1.1715 after opening at BRL1.1723, up from the closing Wednesday of BRL1.1700. \par \par \uc2  But another trader in Sao Paulo said it wasn't clear if the Banco do Brasil was operating for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "When you act for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  it is to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Banco do Brasil was both selling and buying dollars," the Sao Paulo trader said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  desk at the Banco do Brasil wasn't immediately available to comment on its role in the market Thursday. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires  13-08-98 \par \par \uc2  2028GMT \par \par \uc2  1156 1843 \par \par \uc2 Dokument em00000020010917du8d0073a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
