\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Brazil stocks hit 9-mo. low,  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  steady. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shasta Darlington \par \uc2 558 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 1998\par 00:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO, Aug 11 (Reuters) - Brazilian markets were rocked on Tuesday after more unfavourable economic news from abroad, with stocks plunging to a nine-month low and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stumbling, then regaining its feet amid reports of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The blue-chip Bovespa index tumbled 4.14 percent after more overnight market instability in Russia and Japan. The slide marked the Brazilian index's eighth consecutive day in negative territory and extended its losses for the year to more than 13 percent. \par \par \uc2  The Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, held up despite morning nervousness. Brokers speculated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  by indirectly selling dollars to offset any perception that \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  was facing mounting pressure to devaluate its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Brazil's real is closely watched in times of market upheaval, as the country shares some of the traits of the Southeast Asian countries that were forced to devalue last fall: huge fiscal deficits, a trade gap and a closely regimented \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  A sharp devaluation could likely trigger a regional crisis that would dwarf the meltdown of the Mexican peso in 1994. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday morning, the tightly controlled Brazilian real jerked lower against the dollar in spot and futures trade, but it bounced back before lunch. \par \par \uc2  Officials pointed to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stability as a sign that pressure from abroad would be limited to the stock market. \par \par \uc2  "For the time being, this is an equities phenomenon. It has not had any affect on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is what is most important," a Finance Ministry spokesman said. \par \par \uc2  Still, dollar traders attributed the real's strong rebound to heavy dollar-selling by federally-owned Banco do Brasil on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The real is widely regarded to be overvalued by between 10 percent and 30 percent. Economists say Brazil may not be able to resist devaluation should China take that option in response to an ever-weakening Japanese yen. \par \par \uc2  The Brazilian real opened 0.16 percent weaker at 1.1708 reais per dollar from 1.1689 at Monday's close. But it strengthened to 1.1688 reais by the market's close. \par \par \uc2  "The market opened very nervous, but it is a lot calmer now. There are rumours Banco do Brasil was selling dollars for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which they have done in the past," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at an international bank. \par \par \uc2  The speculation was backed by the fact that there was a net outflow of dollars from Brazil on Tuesday, a phenomenon that usually weakens the real. \par \par \uc2  Traders said Banco do Brasil may have sold up to $300 million Tuesday to bolster the real. By comparison, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold between $8 billion and $10 billion to support the real during one week last October and November at the height of the Asian crisis. \par \par \uc2  Brazil, in contrast with many emerging nations, has a huge foreign reserve war chest of around $70 billion, and thus is better able to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not comment on the Banco do Brasil selling rumours or the declining stock market. "If anyone says anything, it will be at the end of day," a \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spokesman said in Brasilia. \par \par \uc2  ((Shasta Darlington, Sao Paulo newsroom, 5511-248-5408, shasta.darlington@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8b02xax\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
