\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil Forex \highlight\uc2  -3:  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 's End Seen At BRL1.1685/Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2 200 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 1998\par 23:29\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  At the close of business in the spot market, the dollar for 48-hour delivery ended with a buy rate of BRL1.1685 and a sell rate of BRL1.1687, leaving the midpoint average at BRL1.16860, virtually unchanged from Monday's BRL1.16880. \par \par \uc2  The dollar shot higher against the real at opening, hitting a sell rate of BRL1.1710 - from BRL1.1689 at Monday's close - on yet another bout of pessimism over Russia, Japan and China's ability to keep the yuan from devaluing. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's C-bonds - the country's most liquid debt instrument - fell 3 1/8 in early New York trade, before rebounding and ending the session higher. Equities collapsed. The Sao Paulo Stock Exchange's Bovespa Index fell as much as 5.7% before ending the day 4.1% lower. \par \par \uc2  "The market opened very nervous today, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was putting out fires everywhere, in the spot and futures markets," another Rio de Janeiro dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Asked to qualify the size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , one dealer said, "It was enough." \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 11-08-98 \par \par \uc2  2129GMT \par \par \uc2  1156 1843 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8b06ski\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
