\par\par \b \uc2  REPEAT:WSJ: Leveraged Players Spark Brazil Markets \b0\par\par\uc2 605 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 1997\par 14:19\par \uc2  Emerging Markets Report \par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  From Friday's Wall Street Journal \par \par \uc2  By Thomas T. Vogel, Jr. \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The unabated turmoil in Brazilian financial markets is a lesson in how Latin America's slickest financiers became victims of their own excess. \par \par \uc2  The Sao Paulo stock exchange plunged 9.8% Thursday while the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the third time this week as big losses by some highly leveraged bond players sparked a chain reaction across the country's financial markets. \par \par \uc2  Thursday, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at least two times to support the value of the real, which has come under pressure in the wake of the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis. A plunge in Hong Kong's stock exchange last week and fears of a devaluation of the Hong Kong dollar, once considered one of the globe's strongest \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sparked a world-wide markets sell-off which continues to plague Latin America. The pressure has been most acute on Brazil, the region's biggest economy. \par \par \uc2  Local Brazilian dealers say the government may have spent as much as $2 billion Thursday to keep the real from slipping out of its crawling devaluation band pegged at 0.6% a month. This comes after it had reportedly shelled out as much as $5 billion on Tuesday and hundreds of millions more on Monday to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . At the end of September, Brazil's foreign reserves stood at $61 billion. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  called an emergency meeting of its monetary policy committee late Thursday and decided to sharply raise key short-term interest rates. Brazil's monthly prime rate, for example, was raised to 3.05% from 1.58%. Higher rates could help ease pressure on the real by making investments in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  more attractive and making it more expensive to speculate. But it could also increase strains on the banking system. \par \par \uc2  "They feel that the real is under serious attack and they're hiking interest rates very sharply. I still wouldn't call them desperate at this stage, but clearly these are drastic measures which, if left in place for long, would create serious concerns over the impact on the financial sector," said Jene Frieda, senior Latin America economist at think tank IDEA Inc. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also offered to buy back several hundred million in short-term Brazilian notes denominated in reals several months before their maturity dates, a move designed to support the real. \par \par \uc2  As with many central banks, Brazil's top bank doesn't give out information on its market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But local dealers can estimate how much the bank spent through informal surveys of a group of 60 financial institutions the government uses to buy or sell foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Fallout from the Asian crisis has been more acute in Brazil than in any other Latin nation partly because of what many considered an overvalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and partly because of the aggressiveness of Brazilian financial players. Brazil has long had a reputution for having the most agile markets in the world, a legacy of the past three decades when rampant inflation forced traders to move very quickly to stay one step ahead. \par \par \uc2  "Brazil is a much more sophisticated investment-banking market than anywhere else in Latin America," said Igor Cornelsen, a merchant banker in Sao Paulo. "There are a lot of sophisticated banks and as a consequence, a lot of leveraged funds," he explained. "So when the big crisis came and there were margin calls, some funds . . . lost 50 to 60%." \par \par \uc2  (MORE) DOW JONES EMERGING MARKETS REPORT  31-10-97 \par \par \uc2  1319GMT \par \par \uc2  1802 1805 1808 1840 \par \par \uc2 Dokument em00000020011006dtav00dij\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
