\par\par \b \uc2  Merrill Lynch's Latin American Strategists Are Bullish On Brazil \b0\par\par\uc2 560 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 1997\par 20:46\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Merrill Lynch & Co.'s Latin American strategists Friday said the troubled Brazilian stock market is the investment bank's top pick for the next three months despite the recent sell-off. \par \par \uc2  Strategists Ed Cabrera, Renato Grandmont and Robert Berges in a report said the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent decision to double interest rates is likely to attract foreign capital inflows and help unwind speculative dollar positions, which would further build foreign reserves. \par \par \uc2  "Given the sharp sell-off and the negative expectations for Brazil, we are encouraged by the proactive policy actions and the strong political will of the (President Fernando Henrique) Cardoso administration to protect the real," Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised two key interest rates late Thursday to stem speculative attacks on the the real. Its Basic Rate (TBC), or prime rate, was raised to 3.05% from 1.58% and its Assistance Rate, at which money is lent to banks, was raised to 3.23%, from 1.76%. \par \par \uc2  Reaction among market watchers was divided. While some expected the rate increase to calm the fears of foreign investors, others focused on the possibility that the higher rates could lead to a recession. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, Merrill Lynch cautioned that certain risks remain in the Brazilian market, including the possibility that high interest rates won't reduce capital outflows or that they will lead to bankruptcies in the banking industry. \par \par \uc2  "However, expected government action should reduce the risks," they said. "We do not expect a devaluation to occur." \par \par \uc2  Planning Minister Antonio Kandir said in a radio interview Friday that the elevated interest rates would only be in place until the market turmoil subsides. \par \par \uc2  The Merrill Lynch analysts noted that \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to protect the ceiling of the real's exchange-rate band and it's estimated that international reserves declined by nearly $5 billion, from $62 billion, Merrill Lynch said. \par \par \uc2  While high interest rates will encourage foreign flows into debt markets and away from equities, the strategists contend the equity market is "equally attractive." Brazilian stocks, they said, could rally 45% in several weeks if the risk of devaluation is removed. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's stock market rose 1.5% Friday after plunging 9.8% Thursday. The benchmark Bovespa index for the Sao Paulo exchange - South America's largest market - has fallen a total of 22% in the last week. \par \par \uc2  For Brazil, the strategists recommend badly depressed, highly liquid, public sector stocks undergoing privatizations such as Telecomunicacoes Brasileiras SA (TBR) and Cemig (CEMGY). These stocks, they said, are attractively priced. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the Merrill analysts reduced their recommended exposure to the smaller and outperforming markets of Chile and Colombia, while adding weight to Mexico. \par \par \uc2  Mexico's stocks and the peso are likely to rally once the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  turmoil in Brazil stabilizes, the strategists said. Top stock picks in Mexico include consumer-related companies such as retail giant Grupo Cifra SA (CFRAY), Coca-Cola Femsa (KOF) and Telefonos de Mexico (TMX). \par \par \uc2  "Mexico remains a fundamentally solid story," they said. \par \par \uc2  Merrill Lynch also is "overweight" - temporarily exceeding its normal investment allocations - in Argentina. Its favored stocks there include Telefonica de Argentina (TAR) and YPF SA (YPF). \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1997 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020011006dtav00zte\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
