\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2 / \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  -2:  \highlight22\uc2 Interventions \highlight\uc2  To Avoid Speculation \b0\par\par\uc2 199 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 August 1996\par 23:37\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  A dealer said the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were necessary due to speculating involving the Argentine economy. \par \par \uc2  'Buyers were afraid of a possible devaluation in Argentina, which could pressure Brazil to follow suit,' the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  At the closing, dealers said the buy rate was 1.096 reals per dollar and the sell rate was 1.098 reals per dollar, little changed from Wednesday's rate. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the commercial market to alter the 'mini-band' to a buy rate of 1.010 reals per dollar and a sell rate of 1.015 reals per dollar. That was a move downward of 0.2 centavo from the previous mini-band rate. \par \par \uc2  The mini-band sits within a larger \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  band, last adjusted on Jan. 30 to 97 centavos-1.06 real per dollar. \par \par \uc2  That move was seen as a continuation of the policy to allow the real to continue its gradual devaluation against the dollar. Since March 1995, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has carried out regular mini-devaluations of the real to nudge the real slowly downward in small increments. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 01-08-96 \par \par \uc2  2137GMT \par \par \uc2  1156 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011013ds8106cd4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
